diag(pip): Record the thermal optic's engine state once per aim, and point the next test at the dump

The camera motion blur is ruled out (turning it off changed nothing), the third hypothesis against
this artifact. What the failures have narrowed is worth recording: the thermal image is computed in
the pack's shader from the G-buffer (infrared(gbuffer_load_data(...))), so no post process effect can
be its source - the fog, the rain, the bloom and the motion blur all operate on the colour image
afterwards. That leaves either something that changes the pixels the sight is drawn into (the
temporal upscaler) or an input of the shader itself.

Two things are needed from the next session and both are now in place.

One, the engine's state for the thermal case, once per aim rather than behind the debug switch: the
answer to "did the stable overlay clause fire" was only ever visible in a debug session, which the
previous commit then turned off by default. One line per aim is affordable and it says whether
unstable=1 for the optic whose image ghosts, along with the ADS progress, the zoom factor, the motion
blur value and the image type.

Two, an image. The dump built for exactly this case (a thermal optic has no lens, so the publish
block never runs for it) writes the main view to pip_optics_screen.bmp with r__svp_debug 2, throttled
to every 600 frames. Panning with the sight up should land one while the artifact is in the frame,
and its shape - a circle, a band, a gradient edge - identifies the mechanism in a way that reading
code has not.